  "# ADR-2607198300: Repository-wide security assurance model

## Context

A scalar 0–100 average is useful for prioritization but can be mistaken for a
probability of safety. It also hides a critical zero behind strong unrelated
controls. Stars and letter grades have the same problem when presented alone.

## Decision

Every kotoba-lang repository security report must include six independent
fields: weighted Security Control Maturity, L0–L4 maturity, S–F grade,
release gate, E0–E5 evidence, and residual critical-gap count.

The machine-readable rule in kotoba-lang/security defines weights, levels,
heatmap bands and critical caps. Any critical control below 40 caps grade at C;
below 20 forces a failed release gate. Missing signed production evidence or
an unresolved critical gap prevents a pass.

The heatmap is mandatory for stack-wide reports, but its colors are navigation
only. Neither a score, grade, stars nor green cell authorizes release.
Likelihood × impact × exposure remains a separate residual-risk calculation.

## Consequences

Reports become less compressible but cannot conceal catastrophic gaps in an
average. Score changes require executable evidence; production qualification
requires fresh authority-signed receipts. Accepted non-goals remain explicit
gray/N/A cells rather than silently improving or reducing a release decision."}]
